{"ts": 1789685528.103632, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "I used up all 20 evaluations in one batch (I should have been more careful, but I have good data). Feature 10 (long) gave the best single-feature Sharpe of 0.450, with feature 13 (short, since it was -0.411, so shorting gives +0.411) also strong, and feature 1, 6, 8, 15 also solid (~0.3+).\n\nSince I have no more evaluate calls left, I'll combine the best-looking features into a 3-feature equal-weight spec based on economic/statistical reasoning: feature 10 (long, 0.450), feature 13 (short, 0.411), and feature 8 (long, 0.309) \u2014 these were the three strongest single-feature signals. Combining diverse strong signals equal-weight often reduces variance and can improve Sharpe if they're not too correlated, but there's real risk of overlap/correlation reducing the benefit, plus in-sample Sharpes are optimistic versus OOS.\n\nI'll submit this combination with a conservative OOS Sharpe estimate, since single-feature in-sample Sharpes ~0.3-0.45 typically shrink substantially out-of-sample (multiple testing/overfitting on top of already limited signal), and combining doesn't guarantee more than modest improvement without in-sample validation of the combo itself."}
